B.RA=s(["Prodromal high fever, chills, and intense asthenia","Marked painful regional lymphadenopathy prior to or concurrent with rash","Distinct central lesion umbilication with firm indurated bases","Palmar and plantar involvement in over 70% of classical presentations","Painful proctitis or pharyngitis when mucosal sites are infected"],t.s)
B.Qc=s(['Vs Chickenpox: Mpox lesions are synchronous and deep-seated with prominent lymphadenopathy; Chickenpox is pleomorphic ("dewdrops") with mild/absent lymphadenopathy.',"Vs Cowpox: Cowpox is typically a solitary or paucifocal large black eschar, whereas Mpox is generalized/clustered multi-lesion.","Vs HFMD: HFMD lesions are non-umbilicated, flat or oval grey-white blisters on palms/soles without prominent cervical/inguinal lymphadenopathy."],t.s)
B.PJ=s(["Ocular involvement or keratitis (risk of corneal scarring/blindness)","Secondary bacterial soft-tissue sepsis or confluent cellulitis","Severe pharyngeal edema causing airway compromise","Altered mental status, encephalopathy, or severe hemodynamic collapse"],t.s)
B.V1=s(["assets/images/samples/mpox_sample_1.jpg","assets/images/samples/mpox_sample_2.jpg"],t.s)
B.Jy=new A.et("mpox","Mpox (Monkeypox)","Monkeypox Virus (MPXV)","Poxviridae (Orthopoxvirus genus)","Firm, deep-seated, well-circumscribed papulovesicular lesions advancing to tense pustules with pronounced central umbilication, eventual thick dark crusting and scarring.","Synchronous progression in any anatomical zone: Macules (1-2d) -> Papules (1-2d) -> Vesicles (1-2d) -> Pustules (5-7d) -> Desiccation/Crusting (7-14d).","Centrifugal predilection: initial lesions frequently appear on face, oral mucosa, palms and soles, and perianal/genital mucosal boundaries.","Prominent, painful, firm lymphadenopathy (submandibular, cervical, axillary, or inguinal). Hall-mark clinical discriminator against Varicella.",B.RA,B.Qc,B.PJ,"Level 1: High Priority","Strict Airborne & Contact Precautions in an airborne infection isolation room (AIIR) or single room. Full PPE (fit-tested N95, fluid-resistant gown, eye protection, gloves).",0.84,0.7778,0.8077,27,B.V1)
B.To=s(["Mild prodromal low-grade fever, malaise, and anorexia in children","Successive crops of intensely pruritic polymorphic lesions","Rapid evolution from tear-drop vesicle to rupture within 8-12 hours","Absence of deep-seated induration or classical central umbilication","Mucosal shallow erosions in palate or genital mucosa"],t.s)
B.S4=s(["Vs Mpox: Multiple stages of lesions present simultaneously (pleomorphism); palms and soles typically spared; lymphadenopathy is minimal.","Vs Measles: Measles rash is non-vesicular (morbilliform maculopapular) without fluid-filled blisters.","Vs HFMD: HFMD lesions predominate on hands, feet, and mouth rather than generalized trunk distribution."],t.s)
B.TX=s(["Secondary invasive Streptococcus or Staphylococcus superinfection (necrotizing fasciitis)","Varicella pneumonia (especially in adults, smokers, or pregnant females)","Cerebellar ataxia or post-infectious encephalitis","Aspirin administration leading to Reye Syndrome risk (strictly avoid salicylates)"],t.s)
B.UB=s(["assets/images/samples/chickenpox_sample_1.jpg","assets/images/samples/chickenpox_sample_2.jpg"],t.s)
B.JB=new A.et("chickenpox","Chickenpox (Varicella)","Varicella-Zoster Virus (VZV)","Herpesviridae (Alphaherpesvirinae)",'Superficial thin-walled clear vesicles on an erythematous base classically described as "dewdrops on a rose petal", rapidly progressing to pustules and crusts.',"Pleomorphic / Asynchronous presentation: new crops appear in successive waves over 3 to 5 days, resulting in simultaneous coexistence of macules, papules, intact vesicles, and crusted lesions on the same body region.","Centripetal distribution: commences on scalp, face, and trunk before sparingly extending to proximal extremities; rarely involves palms and soles.","Absent to mild, generalized, non-tender lymph node enlargement.",B.To,B.S4,B.TX,"Level 3: Standard","Airborne and contact isolation until all vesicles have completely dried and formed firm dry crusts (minimum 5-7 days from rash onset).",0.6667,0.6667,0.6667,9,B.UB)
B.SQ=s(["Intense prodrome with high spiking fever (> 39.5 C / 103 F)",'The Classic "Three Cs": Cough (barking), Coryza (severe rhinitis), and Conjunctivitis (photophobia)',"Pathognomonic Koplik spots: tiny 1-2 mm blue-white spots with red halos on buccal mucosa (precedes rash by 24-48h)",'Confluent facial rash giving a characteristic "measly" facies',"Fine branny desquamation during recovery phase"],t.s)
B.TL=s(["Vs Mpox & Chickenpox: Entirely maculopapular without vesicular fluid; lack of pustules or umbilicated crusts.","Vs Cowpox: Generalized confluent morbilliform pattern rather than localized ulcerating necrotic eschar.","Vs Drug exanthem: Presence of Koplik spots, high fever, and photophobic conjunctivitis."],t.s)
B.QT=s(["Severe secondary bronchopneumonia (leading cause of measles mortality)","Acute measles encephalitis or subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE)","Severe corneal ulceration and xerophthalmia (prevented by high-dose Vitamin A)","Severe dehydration from persistent diarrhea"],t.s)
B.Sx=s(["assets/images/samples/measles_sample_1.jpg","assets/images/samples/measles_sample_2.jpg"],t.s)
B.Jz=new A.et("measles","Measles (Rubeola)","Measles Morbillivirus","Paramyxoviridae","Confluent erythematous maculopapular (morbilliform) rash that starts discrete and coalesces into large irregular patches; resolves with coppery hyperpigmentation and fine desquamation.","Non-vesicular cephalocaudal wave: originates behind ears and along hairline, descends over face and neck, then sweeps over trunk and extremities within 3 days.","Cephalocaudal pattern (Head -> Neck -> Torso -> Arms/Legs). Palmar and plantar involvement is uncommon.","Mild generalized lymphadenopathy, prominent post-auricular and occipital nodes.",B.SQ,B.TL,B.QT,"Level 2: Urgent","Immediate airborne isolation (negative pressure room, N95 respiratory protection for all staff). Reportable immediately to public health authorities.",0.8571,1,0.9231,6,B.Sx)
B.TW=s(["History of rodent contact or scratch/bite from outdoor domestic cat","Profound localized erythema, edema, and induration surrounding solitary eschar","Systemic symptoms including low-grade fever, malaise, and localized tenderness","Central dark necrotic depression surrounded by a ring of tense inflammatory vesicles","Protracted healing course requiring weeks to complete re-epithelialization"],t.s)
B.Un=s(["Vs Cutaneous Anthrax: Anthrax eschars are painless with non-pitting gelatinous edema; Cowpox lesions are exquisitely painful with marked acute inflammation.","Vs Mpox: Cowpox is localized/solitary with a prominent black necrotic eschar; Mpox is generalized and multifocal.","Vs Orf / Milker's Nodule: Parapoxviruses show distinctive targetoid erythema without deep black hemorrhagic necrosis."],t.s)
B.Tq=s(["Disseminated cowpox (generalized spread) in patients with atopic dermatitis (eczema herpeticum-like)","Severe immunosuppression leading to fulminant necrotizing disease","Ocular cowpox leading to severe blepharoconjunctivitis or panophthalmitis","Secondary pyogenic cellulitis / MRSA superinfection"],t.s)
B.Ro=s(["assets/images/samples/cowpox_sample_1.jpg","assets/images/samples/cowpox_sample_2.jpg"],t.s)
B.JA=new A.et("cowpox","Cowpox","Cowpox Virus (CPXV)","Poxviridae (Orthopoxvirus genus)","Solitary or paucifocal (1 to 3) lesions starting as an inflamed macule, transforming into a hard indurated papule, hemorrhagic vesicle, and ultimately a large deeply excavated black necrotic eschar.","Slow localized progression over 3 to 6 weeks: Papule -> Hemorrhagic Bulla -> Central Necrosis -> Thick Black Eschar (Anthrax-like) -> Scarring.","Focal and asymmetrical: almost exclusively on inoculation sites such as fingers, hands, forearms, or face.","Severe, exquisitely painful regional lymphadenitis and localized ascending lymphangitis.",B.TW,B.Un,B.Tq,"Level 1: High Priority","Contact precautions. Occlusive dressing over necrotic lesion. Healthcare workers should wear gloves and gown during wound dressing changes.",0.75,0.8571,0.8,7,B.Ro)
B.PC=s(["Pediatric predilection (primarily infants and children under 5 years)","Painful intraoral vesicles that rapidly ulcerate, leading to dysphagia and refusal to feed","Elliptical or football-shaped vesicles on palms and soles that run parallel to Langer lines","Low-grade fever (38-39 C) lasting 24-48 hours","Late complication of painless onychomadesis (nail shedding) 4-8 weeks post-recovery"],t.s)
B.Sw=s(["Vs Mpox: HFMD blisters are small, non-umbilicated, painless or mildly tender, and self-limiting without significant lymphadenopathy.","Vs Chickenpox: Lesions confined primarily to palms, soles, and mouth; lack of generalized centripetal pleomorphic crops on torso.","Vs Aphthous Stomatitis: Regular aphthae lack concomitant palm and sole cutaneous vesicles."],t.s)
B.UQ=s(["EV-A71 associated brainstem encephalitis, aseptic meningitis, or acute flaccid paralysis","Neurogenic pulmonary edema or cardio-respiratory collapse","Severe dehydration in young toddlers due to painful oral enanthem","High persistent fever > 39 C for more than 3 consecutive days"],t.s)
B.T7=s(["assets/images/samples/hfmd_sample_1.jpg","assets/images/samples/hfmd_sample_2.jpg"],t.s)
B.Jx=new A.et("hfmd","Hand, Foot, and Mouth Disease (HFMD)","Coxsackievirus A16, Enterovirus A71 (EV-A71), Coxsackievirus A6","Picornaviridae (Enterovirus genus)","Small (2-5 mm), oval or football-shaped greyish-white papulovesicles oriented along skin tension lines, surrounded by a thin erythematous halo.","Rapid resolution: maculopapules evolve into discrete non-pruritic blisters that do not crust or scar, resolving spontaneously within 7 to 10 days.","Characteristic acral predilection: palms, soles, interdigital spaces, buttocks, knees, and painful intraoral ulcerations (herpangina on tongue and buccal mucosa).","Generally absent or mild cervical reactive nodes secondary to oral ulcerations.",B.PC,B.Sw,B.UQ,"Level 3: Standard","Contact and enteric precautions. Exclusion from daycare/school until oral ulcers have healed and child has been afebrile for 24 hours. Rigorous hand hygiene.",0.9333,0.875,0.9032,16,B.T7)
B.UW=s(["Intact epidermal surface with preserved skin furrow patterns","Absence of inflammatory hypervascularity or perilesional erythema","Absence of microvesicles, purulent exudate, or crusting","Normal capillary refill time (< 2 seconds)","Serves as negative control baseline for clinical screening accuracy"],t.s)
B.UM=s(["Essential specificity benchmark in the MSLD v2.0 dataset to minimize false positive referrals.","Ensures that non-pathological skin tones and lighting variations are not misdiagnosed as infectious exanthems."],t.s)
B.MN=s(["Presence of newly developed rapidly expanding pigmented lesions (ABCDE melanoma screening)","Sudden onset petechiae or non-blanching purpura (meningococcemia screen)"],t.s)
B.Sy=s(["assets/images/samples/healthy_sample_1.jpg","assets/images/samples/healthy_sample_2.jpg"],t.s)
B.JC=new A.et("healthy","Healthy Skin (Control Baseline)","None (Physiological Baseline)","Intact Cutaneous Micro-Environment","Smooth, intact epidermal architecture with uniform pigmentation, normal skin turgor, and complete absence of inflammatory erythema, papules, pustules, or ulcerations.","Stable physiological epidermal renewal; normal barrier function without active immunological cascade.","Generalized intact cutaneous envelope across all anatomical dermatomes.","Normal, non-palpable, non-tender lymph nodes.",B.UW,B.UM,B.MN,"Level 4: Routine","No isolation or infection control restrictions required. Patient reassurance and guidance on general skin hygiene.",0.8,0.8889,0.8421,9,B.Sy)
